Do not use __attribute__((model)) on LoongArch.

brotli selects the attribute on a COMPILER VERSION test:

    #if BROTLI_GNUC_HAS_ATTRIBUTE(model, 3, 0, 3)
    #define BROTLI_MODEL(M) __attribute__((model(M)))

clang answers __has_attribute(model) affirmatively -- it implements the
attribute for the targets that have code models, such as m68k -- and then
rejects the value on a target that has none:

    c/common/context.c:6:20: error: code model 'small' is not supported
                                    on this target

so every translation unit that declares a large constant table fails, and
libbrotlicommon does not build for loongarch64. GCC does not hit this because
its loongarch backend accepts the attribute.

The attribute is an optimisation hint about where constant data is placed. It
has no effect on correctness and dropping it on one target costs nothing
measurable here.

Affects loongarch64 only; the other four targets build unpatched.

--- a/c/common/platform.h
+++ b/c/common/platform.h
@@ -665,7 +665,13 @@
 #undef BROTLI_TEST
 #endif
 
-#if BROTLI_GNUC_HAS_ATTRIBUTE(model, 3, 0, 3)
+/* BROTLI_GNUC_HAS_ATTRIBUTE tests the compiler, not the target. clang
+   accepts the attribute name on every target and then rejects the code-model
+   value on targets that have no code models, so the compiler test alone is
+   not sufficient. LoongArch is such a target under clang. */
+#if defined(__loongarch__) && defined(__clang__)
+#define BROTLI_MODEL(M) /* M: no code models on this target under clang */
+#elif BROTLI_GNUC_HAS_ATTRIBUTE(model, 3, 0, 3)
 #define BROTLI_MODEL(M) __attribute__((model(M)))
 #else
 #define BROTLI_MODEL(M) /* M */
